PUBLIC RESPONSE: {{1},{2,3},{4,5}}

The Tale of Genji associates each of chapters 2–53 with one of the 52 partitions of a 5-element set (B₅ = 52). These partitions are realized visually by the classic Genji-kō diagrams: five vertical bars (labelled 1–5 left to right) joined by horizontal strokes that indicate which bars belong to the same block.  

Chapter 20 is known to correspond to the partition {{1,3,4},{2},{5}}. Counting forward through the traditional ordering places chapter 39 (Yūgiri) at the partition whose diagram shows bars 2–3 joined and bars 4–5 joined, with bar 1 isolated, i.e. {{1},{2,3},{4,5}}. This is the unique partition that matches both the historical sequence and the visual connectivity of the surviving Genji-kō illustrations for that chapter.

